WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.760
Now I would like to share with you

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.540
how the materials we work on make it onto Amruta.

3
00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:18.550
When we have finished the work on a certain

4
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.840
translation...

5
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.240
How does it happen? For example, this one is already gone.

6
00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:35.500
When we have finished the work and our suggestions, corrections

7
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.900
are accepted, or we simply reviewed it and all is well, then we,

8
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.740
right here,

9
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.140
in this issue,

10
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.940
click "Close issue".

11
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.820
When we click it,

12
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:58.820
our material is automatically marked as done on this portal.

13
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:04.380
And it disappears, generally speaking, even from this review list.

14
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.460
After that it can only be seen in "Expert mode".

15
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:15.200
That is, only by clicking "approved" at the bottom do we see this material, we can

16
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.940
look at it, open it and so on.

17
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.400
But when

18
00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:30.530
this material is marked as done... Let's find one that has already been

19
00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.470
marked this way.

20
00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:37.190
That will make it clearer.

21
00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.610
Here, for example, "Raksha Bandhan and Maryadas" has been marked.

22
00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.730
That's it: approved, closed.

23
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.180
After that, nothing is published to Amruta automatically.

24
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.860
Because subtitles and transcripts are handed over to Amruta by e-mail,

25
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:05.460
by correspondence. For now we deliberately don't automate this step, because it is live

26
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:11.440
communication with living yogis. Let me just show you an example

27
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.340
for this talk,

28
00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.890
of what we do.

29
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:27.220
In my case: I take my personal e-mail and send to help@amruta

30
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:34.220
.org, with a copy to the coordinating committee, say, in Ukraine, a letter with

31
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.780
a description and attachments.

32
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:44.440
That is, I describe that for this talk, with such-and-such a link, we want to pass on

33
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:50.850
these corrections. In this case we passed on some small

34
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:56.930
corrections, just spacing: a space missing here, an extra one there, in the English

35
00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:01.310
transcript. One was corrected...

36
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:09.070
One small mistake in a word in the Ukrainian transcript, and we also added, because

37
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.650
there were no English or Ukrainian subtitles.

38
00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:19.130
So this is a manual step, and at this

39
00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:25.050
final stage, so far only I have been doing it.

40
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:31.290
If anyone would like to take on this phase, that is very welcome,

41
00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:38.210
and most likely it will even become an urgent need as the number

42
00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:41.430
of people proofreading translations grows.

43
00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:47.030
Yes, because if the final step rests on just one person, it

44
00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:51.930
may take... It will be a bottleneck, right?

45
00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.730
Performance will suffer,

46
00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.480
the productivity of this step.

47
00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:07.350
And when we send it, they reply: here you are, done, with

48
00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:13.370
a link, everything is updated. After that I

49
00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:19.950
write a message to the team that we have a new

50
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:25.650
translated material, and with that the work is complete.

51
00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:33.130
Once it reaches Amruta, it is automatically synced with YouTube, with Vimeo,

52
00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:37.090
and on Amruta itself, of course, everything is synced.

53
00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:43.230
And this material of ours is now in free flight, sailing

54
00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:50.210
freely. Anyone can use it, and if excerpts are made,

55
00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:56.360
our Ukrainian subtitles will be available there too.

56
00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:02.020
After that the transcript is also available on Amruta.

57
00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:10.460
Until this whole process with Amruta has happened, and you need to use

58
00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:17.080
the transcript, you can always find it in our repository, in talks,

59
00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:23.540
by opening transcript_uk.txt,

60
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:24.840
and get it.

61
00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.480
That's all!
